  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough should become negatively tilted over the \nFour Corners region by Friday night. This will place the region in\nan active southwest flow aloft through Sunday. A warm and moist \nlow level airmass will also persist with dewpoints in the upper \n60s. For now, there is low (<30%) chance for thunderstorms Friday \nevening. Storms should be fairly scattered in nature and initiate \nalong a dryline in the Permian Basin then push into our western \ncounties. SPC has a Marginal Risk of severe storms across our \nwestern counties for Friday, with strong wind gusts and hail being\nthe main concerns. A similar setup is expected for Saturday and \nSunday. As a result, additional rounds of thunderstorms are \nexpected for Saturday night and Sunday night with otherwise near-\nnormal temperatures.\n\nThe main upper-level trough over the western US should lift to \nthe northern Plains starting Monday. The pattern will become a bit\nmurky for the first half of next week. While models show some \nridging building in across Texas, they also show a weak upper low \nsetting up across the state. Models also show a \"back-door\" \nfrontal boundary moving through central Texas Monday through \nWednesday. This would lead to more chances for showers and \nthunderstorms during this time. However, given the uncertainty of \ntiming and location of these key features, overall rain chances \nwere kept low as storms should be scattered in nature.",
